  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\n\nAnother H5 wave rides the southwest flow on Wednesday, this time \ntracking along the South Dakota/Nebraska border area into western \nMinnesota. A sfc low spins up and tracks northeast of the area \nplacing us in the warm sector. Will see a plume of 500-1,000 J/kg of \ninstability overspread portions of eastern Nebraska by Wednesday \nafternoon ahead of an approaching sfc cold front. A few storms could \nignite by that timeframe, but any organized severe threat doesn't \nappear probable at this time. The best 0-6km shear appears located \nwell north of the plume, and the best synoptic scale forcing and \nheight falls are located in the Dakotas and Minnesota. Machine \nlearning guidance at the minimum suggests a low 5% chance we could \nsee a strong to severe storm. PoPs during this timeframe remain at \n20 to 40%, peaking to 50 to 70% by Wednesday evening across far \neastern Nebraska into southwest Iowa.\n\nOur active pattern continues through a good chunk of the long term \nperiod as we remain in zonal to southwesterly flow. Expect periodic \nrain chances to continue late Thursday night into Friday (50 to 70% \nchance) as another mid-level wave approaches from the southwest. \nRain lingers into Saturday, while by Sunday another trof approaches \nfrom the Intermountain West. Lots of spread still seen amongst \nguidance though in regards to placement of features.\n\nHighs largely remain in the 60s and 70s for most of the period. \nWednesday appears to be the warmest day with some spots in our far \nsoutheast potentially hitting 80F.",
